[Event "Alekhine: Modern"]

{The Alekhine (1...Nf6) invites White's pawns forward and then attacks them.}
1. e4 {Claims the centre: the pawn on e4 controls d5 and f5. It also opens a line for the queen on d1 and the bishop on f1.}
1... Nf6 {[%cal Gf6e4] The Alekhine. Develops the knight to f6, where it controls d5 and eyes the pawn on e4. That pawn is currently undefended.}
2. e5 {Gains time. Claims the centre: the pawn on e5 controls d6 and eyes the knight on f6.}
2... Nd5 {Central knight. Moves the knight out of attack to d5.}
3. d4 {Claims the centre: the pawn on d4 controls c5 and supports the pawn on e5. It also opens a line for the bishop on c1 and the queen on d1.}
3... d6 {Hits e5. Supports the centre: the pawn on d6 controls c5 and eyes the pawn on e5. It also opens a line for the bishop on c8.}
4. Nf3 {The Modern. Develops the knight to f3, where it supports the pawn on e5 and the pawn on d4.}
4... Bg4 {Pins. Develops the bishop to g4, where it controls f5 and e6. White's last move put a knight on f3, which this now pressures.}
 (4... g6 {Fianchetto. The pawn on g6 controls f5 and h5.}
5. Bc4 {Hits the knight. Develops the bishop to c4, where it eyes the knight on d5. That clears the way to castle kingside.}
5... Nb6 {Moves the knight out of attack to b6 and controls d5. It attacks the bishop on c4, which is undefended.}
6. Bb3 {Moves the bishop out of attack to b3 and controls d5.}
6... Bg7 {Fianchettoes the bishop on g7, where it rakes the long diagonal through f6 and e5. That clears the way to castle kingside.}
7. a4 {Gains space. The pawn on a4 takes b5 away from the opponent.}
)
5. Be2 {Unpins. Develops the bishop to e2, where it controls d3 and supports the knight on f3. That clears the way to castle kingside.}
5... e6 {Supports the centre: the pawn on e6 controls f5 and supports the knight on d5. It also opens a line for the queen on d8.}
6. O-O {Castles. The king is safe and the rook comes into play.}
6... Be7 {Develops the bishop to e7, where it controls f6 and supports the pawn on d6. That clears the way to castle kingside.}
7. c4 {Kicks the knight. Supports the centre: the pawn on c4 controls b5 and eyes the knight on d5. It also opens a line for the queen on d1.}
7... Nb6 {Moves the knight out of attack to b6 and controls d5. White's last move put a pawn on c4, which this now pressures.}
8. h3 {Asks the bishop. The pawn on h3 eyes the bishop on g4. That bishop is currently undefended.}
8... Bh5 {Keeps the pin. Moves the bishop out of attack to h5.}
9. Nc3 {Develops the knight to c3, where it controls d5 and e4.}
9... O-O {Castles. The king is safe and the rook comes into play.}
10. Be3 {Develops the bishop to e3, where it supports the pawn on d4.}
10... d5 {Fixes the centre. The pawn on d5 controls e4 and eyes the pawn on c4. It also opens a line for the bishop on e7.} *

[Event "Alekhine: Four Pawns & 2.Nc3"]

{The Four Pawns Attack is the critical test. 2.Nc3 avoids the theory.}
1. e4 {Claims the centre: the pawn on e4 controls d5 and f5. It also opens a line for the queen on d1 and the bishop on f1.}
1... Nf6 {Alekhine. Develops the knight to f6, where it controls d5 and eyes the pawn on e4. That pawn is currently undefended.}
2. e5 {Gains time. Claims the centre: the pawn on e5 controls d6 and eyes the knight on f6.}
 (2. Nc3 {Avoids the Alekhine. Develops the knight to c3, where it controls d5 and supports the pawn on e4.}
2... e5 {Central reply. Claims the centre: the pawn on e5 controls d4 and f4. It also opens a line for the bishop on f8.}
3. Nf3 {Develops the knight to f3, where it controls d4 and eyes the pawn on e5. That pawn is currently undefended.}
3... Nc6 {Four Knights. Develops the knight to c6, where it controls d4 and supports the pawn on e5.}
)
2... Nd5 {Central. Moves the knight out of attack to d5.}
3. c4 {Kicks. Supports the centre: the pawn on c4 controls b5 and eyes the knight on d5. It also opens a line for the queen on d1.}
3... Nb6 {Moves the knight out of attack to b6 and controls d5. White's last move put a pawn on c4, which this now pressures.}
4. d4 {Claims the centre: the pawn on d4 controls c5 and supports the pawn on e5. It also opens a line for the bishop on c1 and the queen on d1.}
4... d6 {Hits e5. Supports the centre: the pawn on d6 controls c5 and eyes the pawn on e5. It also opens a line for the bishop on c8.}
5. f4 {The Four Pawns. Supports the centre: the pawn on f4 controls g5 and supports the pawn on e5.}
 (5. exd6 {The Exchange variation. Takes the pawn on d6; Black will want to recapture.}
5... cxd6 {Recaptures toward the centre, keeping a solid pawn majority.}
6. Nc3 {Develops the knight to c3, where it controls d5 and e4.}
6... g6 {[%csl Gg7] Fianchetto aiming at d4. The pawn on g6 controls f5 and h5.}
)
5... dxe5 {Takes the pawn on e5; White will want to recapture.}
6. fxe5 {Recaptures on e5, restoring the material balance.}
6... Nc6 {Hits d4. Develops the knight to c6, where it eyes the pawn on d4 and the pawn on e5. White's last move put a pawn on e5, which this now pressures.}
7. Be3 {Develops the bishop to e3, where it supports the pawn on d4.}
7... Bf5 {Develops the bishop to f5, where it controls e4.}
8. Nc3 {Develops the knight to c3, where it controls d5 and e4.}
8... e6 {Supports the centre: the pawn on e6 controls d5 and supports the bishop on f5. It also opens a line for the queen on d8 and the bishop on f8.}
9. Nf3 {Develops the knight to f3, where it supports the pawn on e5 and the pawn on d4.}
9... Be7 {Develops the bishop to e7, where it controls f6 and d6. That clears the way to castle kingside.} *
